# Working behind a corporate proxy or CA

## Symptoms

You are behind a corporate network with a TLS-inspecting proxy, and one or
more of the following fail with a certificate verification error:

- `uv sync`, `uv lock`, `uv add`
- `uv python install`
- Downloads that do not go through uv/pip, such as `huggingface_hub`
  fetching a model or dataset

The error usually mentions `certificate verify failed` or
`unable to get local issuer certificate`.

## Why this happens

The proxy terminates TLS and re-signs traffic with a corporate root CA. That
CA is trusted by your OS (IT installed it there), but uv does not read the
OS trust store by default — it verifies against its own bundled Mozilla
root certificates, which do not include your corporate CA.

The fix is either to make uv trust the OS store, or to point it at a
certificate bundle that includes the corporate CA. Certificate verification
stays on either way — this is not about disabling security checks.

!!! warning
    Do not "fix" this by disabling verification (`allow-insecure-host` /
    `UV_INSECURE_HOST`). See [What not to do](#what-not-to-do) below.

## Per-developer, per-machine fix (recommended)

Set this on your own machine, not in the repository. Create or edit the
user-level uv config:

- Linux/macOS: `~/.config/uv/uv.toml`
- Windows: `%APPDATA%\uv\uv.toml`

```toml
system-certs = true
```

Or, equivalently, without editing a file:

```bash
export UV_SYSTEM_CERTS=true
```

### Why per-machine, not committed to the project

- CI runners (GitHub-hosted, GitLab shared) and Docker builds run outside
  the corporate network and have no corporate CA installed — a project-wide
  setting that assumes one is present would only be a no-op there, but a
  self-hosted runner or an image that *does* need it should get the setting
  at the runner/image level instead (see [CI](#ci) and [Docker](#docker)).
- Other developers on the same project may work outside the proxy (remote,
  a different office, a personal machine) and don't need or want the
  corporate CA trusted by default.

## Explicit bundle alternative

If you would rather not trust the entire OS store, point uv at a specific
PEM bundle:

```bash
export SSL_CERT_FILE=/path/to/bundle.pem
```

!!! warning
    `SSL_CERT_FILE` **replaces** uv's bundled roots entirely — only the
    certificates in that file are trusted. The bundle must contain the
    public CA roots *and* your corporate root, or public package downloads
    will start failing verification.

Build such a bundle on Debian/Ubuntu by concatenating the system roots with
your corporate root:

```bash
cat /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt corp-root.pem > ~/certs/bundle.pem
export SSL_CERT_FILE=~/certs/bundle.pem
```

Other tools have their own equivalent variables and need the same bundle:

- Python `requests`-based tools (including `huggingface_hub`):
  `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` (and `CURL_CA_BUNDLE` for `curl`-backed HTTP clients)
- git and pre-commit: `git config --global http.sslCAInfo /path/to/bundle.pem`

## Project-wide setting

The `system_certs` copier answer, when set to yes, writes
`system-certs = true` into this project's `pyproject.toml` under
`[tool.uv]`. That makes every clone of this project trust the OS
certificate store, with no per-developer setup required.

This is appropriate when **every** developer on the project sits behind the
same corporate proxy — for example, a project that only ever runs on
company-managed laptops. It is a poor fit when contributors work from a mix
of networks (some behind the proxy, some not), since it forces the
assumption onto everyone; use the per-machine fix above instead in that
case.

## What not to do

`allow-insecure-host` (and its environment variable, `UV_INSECURE_HOST`)
disables certificate verification entirely for the listed hosts. The uv
docs are explicit about the risk:

> WARNING: Hosts included in this list will not be verified against the
> system's certificate store and can expose you to security risks due to
> the lack of certificate verification.

The only defensible use is a **host-scoped, temporary** exception for an
internal mirror with a self-signed certificate while you are in the
process of obtaining its CA certificate properly — never as a permanent
fix, and never for a public index such as `pypi.org`. The
`allow_insecure_host` copier answer exists only for that narrow case.

## Private index / mirrors

If your organization mirrors package indexes or model hosting internally,
point the relevant tool at it instead of (or in addition to) fixing
certificates:

- `UV_DEFAULT_INDEX`: replaces the default index (PyPI) for `uv`
- `UV_INDEX`: space-separated list of extra indexes for `uv`
- `UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR`: replaces the GitHub download path uv uses to
  fetch managed Python interpreters
- `HF_ENDPOINT`: replaces the Hugging Face Hub endpoint used by
  `huggingface_hub` / `datasets` / `transformers`

Note that `[[tool.uv.index]]` in `pyproject.toml` points the torch/
torchvision extras at `download.pytorch.org`. If that host is blocked by
your proxy, change those index URLs to your internal proxy repository.

Standard proxy variables are also honored by uv and most Python HTTP
clients: `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY`, `NO_PROXY`.
{% if create_examples %}
## Docker

Drop corporate root CA files (`*.crt`, PEM format) into the `certs/`
directory before running `docker build`. Both build stages install every
`.crt` file found there with `update-ca-certificates` and set
`SSL_CERT_FILE` / `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` accordingly — see `certs/README.md`.

To point the build itself at an internal index or Python mirror, pass
build args:

```bash
docker build \
  --build-arg UV_DEFAULT_INDEX=https://pypi.internal.example.com/simple \
  --build-arg UV_PYTHON_INSTALL_MIRROR=https://python-mirror.internal.example.com \
  -f Dockerfile.package -t {{ project_slug }} .
```
{% endif %}
## CI

GitHub-hosted and GitLab shared runners are not behind your corporate
proxy and need no changes.

Self-hosted runners that *are* behind the proxy should get the trust
setting at the runner/system level rather than in the workflow file:

- `/etc/uv/uv.toml` with `system-certs = true` (applies to every job on
  that runner), or
- `UV_SYSTEM_CERTS=true` set in the runner's own environment

This keeps the workflow file identical for everyone and avoids baking
network assumptions into version-controlled CI config.

